@page "/exchart/grid"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Chart

<DocsPage Product="ExChart" Title="From the grid"
          Lead="A grid's selection becomes a chart from its context menu. The chart follows the rows it was made from: a recalculated value is redrawn, a deleted row becomes a gap with a note, and a click on a point shows its cell.">

    <DocsSection Title="Charting a selection">
        <p>
            <code>GridChartBridge&lt;TRow&gt;</code> takes what the chart needs to know of your rows: how to find
            a row again by its key, each column's Series Kind, and where a column stands along a curve. Its
            <code>Commands</code> join the grid's context menu in one line. Select the rates below and open the
            context menu: a selection with the Date column charts each column along the dates, one of tenor
            columns charts each row as a curve across the tenors. "Add to chart" adds a selection to a chart
            already open, and a selection the chart cannot read is refused with the reason.
        </p>
        <p>
            A level is drawn from zero unless it says otherwise. Rates between 3.0% and 3.6% drawn from zero are
            nearly flat lines, so the declarations say <code>FromZeroOf</code> for each column: <code>false</code>
            reads it away from zero, as a chart built by hand does with <code>FromZero = false</code>. A curve's
            columns share their declaration; a selection that mixes them is refused by name.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under MudBlazor, <code>MudChartDock</code> places each chart in a tab beside the grid or below it,
            never over it, and takes the bridge's events itself. <code>MudChartChrome.GridChrome</code> words the
            bridge's commands in the grid's menu.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>In React.</strong> <code>@@ex-grid/chart-react</code>'s <code>useChartDock</code> (published with
            0.3.0) holds the dock's state: spread its <code>bridgeOptions</code> into the
            <code>GridChartBridge</code>, and hand the state to a dock that draws it.
            <code>@@ex-grid/chart-mantine</code>'s <code>MantineChartDock</code> is that dock in Mantine's tabs, beside
            the grid or below it, never over it: "Chart selection" opens a tab named Chart 1, Chart 2 and so on,
            "Add to chart" offers every open tab, a refusal is said in an alert above the tabs until dismissed, and a
            chart whose axis column has left the grid says so in its tab rather than show what it last drew. Its
            charts take <code>mantineChartChrome</code>, the toolbar, Chart Filters and the context menu in Mantine's
            components, and the theme's primary leads their series in light and dark. A tab not shown keeps its
            chart, with its range and its open Chart Filters.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>@@ex-grid/chart-mui</code>'s <code>MuiChartDock</code> is the same dock in MUI's tabs, its charts
            under <code>muiChartChrome</code>: the toolbar's commands as MUI's buttons with the downloads in a button
            group, Chart Filters in MUI's fields (MUI has no date picker of its own, so a date is typed as the hint
            shows), and the context menu's items as MUI's list item buttons inside the chart's own frame. Its theme is
            made with <code>cssVariables: true</code>, or the dock refuses to draw, naming the option; the primary and
            secondary colours lead the series, as MudBlazor's do under <code>MudChartDock</code>, and error, warning,
            success and info are never a series' colour.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FromGrid)" Mud="typeof(FromGridMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Chart/CurveHistory.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The grid and the charts in the browser">
        <p>
            The bridge charts <code>ExGrid.Components.ExGrid</code>, the grid every Example on this site draws: set
            <code>ScriptGrid</code> with <code>@@ref</code>, and hand the grid's <code>ContextCommands</code> the
            bridge's <code>Commands</code> and its <code>ContextCommandLabel</code> <code>GridChartCommands.Label</code>,
            as the Example above does. Each chart, and each of <code>MudChartDock</code>'s tabs, is
            <code>ExChart.Components.ExChart</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The grid's Context Menu is drawn in the browser, so it asks .NET for the bridge's commands as it opens,
            and opens once they arrive. A key typed in the meantime is held and reaches the menu once it is open. A
            command still running in .NET holds the input that follows it. So a right-click straight after "Chart"
            gets a menu that already offers "Add to chart" for the tab that "Chart" opened, however fast it follows
            and however slow the circuit.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A chart keeps its rows">
        <p>
            A chart is made from rows, not from a picture of them. When the rows change, the chart draws them
            again by their keys; a row that no longer exists leaves a gap and a note, and a row added later is
            never added to a chart made before it. A click on a point moves the grid's Focus to the cell it was
            drawn from, through <code>ShowInGridAsync</code>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
